A||||1||ThatRickGuy||Auto tranny - no shift!?!?||||1||
Z||000000||ThatRickGuy||08-24-2001||01:44 AM||||Hey guys and gals! ran into a bit of a problem tonight  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> car was up and running fine all day, but I went out to a bar to meet some buddies, the car sat for probrably 2 hours or so, and when I took off from there, he shift from first to second was a little hard, and then it wouldn't shift into third. I pulled the selector into second and back to third and it hit third gear, once again, a little harder the usuall. well, I made it home okay, but when I got to my parking lot after a little hill, and she wouldn't go into 2nd. it's too dark to check the fluid, but anyone have any ideas? I only know of 2 minor things it could be, low fluid, or a leaking vacuum line, but I don't have a clue where the vacuum to the tranny is. any help would be greatly appreciated. thanks!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000001||Mach10||08-24-2001||04:24 AM||||2 things spring to mind:<P>1) Kickdown cable is out of adjustment.<BR>2) Something's plugging up your valve bodies. Mine does this every now and then. The best remedy is to stick it in "P" and turn off car for about 5 seconds. This will let the pressure bleed down. I find that "Manual" shifting tends to exaggerate this problem. I'm getting a filter kit done after tuition's paid up. That SHOULD fix it. <P>The hard shifts are probably caused by high line-pressure, either from a plugged valve-body, or a mis-adjusted KD cable<P>Otherwise, the tranny's cacked, and needs rebuilding  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><BR>||||reg||3
